{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Tracking error is the **dispersion** of daily fund-minus-benchmark total returns over the agreed year; tracking difference is their **cumulative gap**. A large error can arise even when the cumulative gap is small, so investigate the daily pattern and its timing—not just year-end performance.\n\n- **Portfolio/construction:** Full replication versus sampling; holdings or weights that diverge from the index; cash drag; delayed rebalances or corporate-action processing; constituent changes, IPOs, or index methodology changes; dividend accrual/reinvestment timing; securities lending; and flows that leave the fund temporarily underinvested or require costly trading.\n- **Pricing/measurement:** Compare like with like—fund NAV total returns against the correct total-return index, in the same currency and with consistent dividend, tax, and valuation conventions. Check stale or fair-valued holdings, market-close mismatches, NAV corrections, distribution treatment, and whether market-price returns were mistakenly compared with index returns. Bid–ask spreads and premiums/discounts affect investor trading returns, not NAV tracking error directly.\n- **Operations/costs:** Trading commissions, spreads, market impact, taxes, and rebalance execution; custody, corporate-action, dividend, and data errors; cash management; fee accruals; valuation processes; and disruptions or delayed settlement.\n\nAttribute deviations by date and source, then distinguish persistent exposure/implementation issues from one-off measurement or operational events."}}
